KingresDB for Debian and Ubuntu
===============================

Layout
------
  /opt/kingres/            the product: bin/ lib/ share/ (server, clients, kingresd)
  /etc/kingres/            kingresd.json (settings), kingresd.env (secrets, root only)
  /var/lib/kingres/        node state, setup token, audit log, certificates (pki/),
                           and the database instance in instances/1/pgdata
                           (instance logs in instances/1/pgdata/log)
  /run/kingres/            Unix sockets of the instances (/run/kingres/1)
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/kingres.service   the one service of the node
  /usr/bin/kingres-*       client programs (see below)

Client programs are linked into /usr/bin with a "kingres-" prefix so that they
cannot collide with the files of the distribution's postgresql-client
packages: kingres-psql, kingres-pg_dump, kingres-pg_dumpall, kingres-pg_restore,
kingres-pg_basebackup, kingres-pg_isready, kingres-pg_config.  The server
programs (postgres, initdb, pg_ctl) are not on the PATH; kingresd uses them
from /opt/kingres/bin.  Also linked: kingresd and kingres-verify.

First run
---------
The service starts after installation in the "uninitialized" state and listens
on 127.0.0.1:8091 only.  A one-time setup token is in a file readable only by
the service user and root:

    sudo cat /var/lib/kingres/setup-token

Open http://127.0.0.1:8091/ on the server (from another machine use an SSH
tunnel:  ssh -L 8091:127.0.0.1:8091 server) and enter the token in the setup
wizard; choose "create cluster" and give the administrator account.  kingresd
then creates the database instance, the administrator role and the system
database, and starts the instance.

Connecting to the database
--------------------------
The instance listens on 127.0.0.1:5432 (settings pg-port and pg-listen).  Log in
as the administrator role created in the wizard:

    kingres-psql -h 127.0.0.1 -p 5432 -U ADMIN postgres

The Unix socket is /run/kingres/1/.s.PGSQL.5432, but local connections on it use
"peer" authentication mapped to the kingresd process only, so use TCP.

Restarting
----------
  restart only the manager (the database keeps running):
      sudo systemctl kill --kill-whom=main --signal=SIGTERM kingres
  restart everything, the database too:
      sudo systemctl restart kingres
  stop everything (the database is shut down with a fast shutdown):
      sudo systemctl stop kingres
After a reboot or "systemctl start" the node comes back as a cluster, without
the wizard.

Removal
-------
  apt remove kingres   stops the service and the database; data and settings stay.
  apt purge kingres    ALSO DELETES /var/lib/kingres (all databases of this
                       node), /etc/kingres and the kingres user.
